};

loadGreeting().then(console.log);

questions.answer:
const loadGreeting = async () => {
  try {
    const mod = await import("./greet.js");
    const greet = mod.default ?? mod.greet;
    return greet("Kakade");
  } catch (err) {
    console.log("load failed:", err.message);
    return "Hello Kakade";
  }
};

loadGreeting().then(console.log);
// Hello Kakade

dynamic import() returns a promise for the module namespace, so await works inside an async function. The default export is mod.default; if a module uses a named export instead, mod.greet covers it. The catch handles network failures, a bad path, or a syntax error in the loaded module. Unlike static import, this call only happens when loadGreeting runs, which is what enables lazy loading and code splitting.

